---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="LightPhotos Blogs"
  desc="Write-ups on why LightPhotos exists and how it's built."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <section class="lp-hero lp-hero-compact">
      <div class="lp-glow"></div>
      <h1 class="lp-wordmark">Blogs</h1>
    </section>

    <ul class="lp-post-list">
      <li>
        <a href="/blogs/my-plan.html">
          <time datetime="2026-09-20">September 20, 2026</time>
          <h2>My plan, and what's not in it</h2>
          <p>Where LightPhotos is going. Your photos stay on your drive, AI runs on your machine, and only the table-stakes features get ported to every platform.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/blogs/measuring-a-gui-app.html">
          <time datetime="2026-09-15">September 15, 2026</time>
          <h2>Measuring a GUI app without lying to yourself</h2>
          <p>You cannot profile a desktop app by clicking around in it, because you never do the same thing twice. Here is the harness I built instead, and the three reports that changed what I worked on.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/blogs/never-load-the-whole-folder.html">
          <time datetime="2026-09-01">September 1, 2026</time>
          <h2>Never load the whole folder</h2>
          <p>A folder of 8,889 photos used 1.9 GB of memory and then crashed. The fix is the most reused idea in the codebase, and I made the same mistake four times.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/blogs/six-queues-and-a-reserved-worker.html">
          <time datetime="2026-08-20">August 20, 2026</time>
          <h2>Six queues and a reserved worker</h2>
          <p>The background decode system in LightPhotos, told as the sequence of problems that produced it. None of the problems are photo-specific.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/blogs/where-your-data-lives.html">
          <time datetime="2026-08-10">August 10, 2026</time>
          <h2>Your data lives where you are allowed to write</h2>
          <p>Why ratings, edits and thumbnails sit in a folder next to your photos instead of in a database, and how a browser's permission rules made that decision for me.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/blogs/what-imageio-gives-you-free.html">
          <time datetime="2026-07-20">July 20, 2026</time>
          <h2>What ImageIO gives you free, and what it costs to leave</h2>
          <p>One call to Apple's ImageIO was doing far more for LightPhotos than I realised. Porting to Linux, Windows and the browser put a number on exactly how much.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/blogs/how-a-photo-gets-on-screen.html">
          <time datetime="2026-07-08">July 8, 2026</time>
          <h2>How a photo gets on screen</h2>
          <p>What actually happens between clicking a photo and seeing it, and all the work the app skips to make that feel instant.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/blogs/learning-rust-while-the-agents-write-it.html">
          <time datetime="2026-07-02">July 2, 2026</time>
          <h2>Learning Rust while the agents write it</h2>
          <p>I picked Rust knowing only the basics, and I still write very little of it by hand. The loop I run around the agents instead, and the four ways it goes wrong.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/blogs/why-rust.html">
          <time datetime="2026-06-28">June 28, 2026</time>
          <h2>Why Rust</h2>
          <p>All Rust, or a Rust engine with a web or Swift UI on top? What I weighed, what I gave up, and the one argument that actually decided it.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/blogs/why-i-started-lightphotos.html">
          <time datetime="2026-06-23">June 23, 2026</time>
          <h2>Why I started LightPhotos</h2>
          <p>A Lightroom license that stopped working, years of photos I couldn't open, and the afternoon I gave up and built the small editor I actually needed.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </main>
</Base>
